"""CS331 Chapter 2 Part 1 (signals, impairments, delays): explaining notes and explained questions, by slide.

NOTES replace a slide's plain notes in order (highlights link to note numbers); extra notes go at the end.
Q replaces the deck's old questions. Tutorial 3 (the solved Physical Layer activity) questions keep their wording.
"""
from .common import pick, truth, tut_written, written

NOTES = {
    1: ['The deck goes from what a signal is, to the two kinds (analog and digital) and how to measure them (frequency and period for analog, bit rate and bit interval for digital), to what goes wrong on the way (attenuation, distortion, noise), to how long delivery takes (propagation, transmission, queuing, latency), and finishes with the bandwidth-delay product and wavelength.',
        'This is the calculation deck, and Tutorial 3 is mostly calculations from it. Most marks are lost on units, not formulas: write the formula, convert everything to base units first (seconds, hertz, bits, metres, bits per second), then calculate, then convert the answer to the unit asked. Remember 1 byte = 8 bits, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 ms = 10⁻³ s, 1 µs = 10⁻⁶ s, and k = 10³, M = 10⁶, G = 10⁹.'],
    2: ['Chapter 2 has six sections. This part covers **2.1 signals** and **2.2 signal impairment**, plus the delay definitions (propagation, transmission, latency) and the bandwidth-delay product. Part 2 covers digital and analog transmission, **multiplexing** (Tutorial 3 asks a scenario on it) and **transmission media**.'],
    3: ['The physical layer defines the **physical characteristics** of the interfaces and the media: how devices are connected to each other and to the link (topology), the shape, size and number of pins on connectors, the voltages and currents, the type of medium (copper, fibre, wireless) and the transmission mode (simplex, half- or full-duplex). You saw this list in Chapter 1 Part 2; this chapter goes into detail.',
        'Three more duties. **Representation of bits**: bits are encoded into electrical or optical signals. **Data rate** (transmission rate, also called bandwidth in this course): how many bits are sent per second. **Synchronisation of bits**: the sender’s and receiver’s clocks must match, so the receiver reads each bit at the right instant.',
        'Devices that work at this layer: the **network card** (its signalling part), the **hub** and the **repeater**. They handle signals only; they never read an address.'],
    4: ['A cable can’t carry the number 1 or the letter A. Data has to be turned into a **signal**, a form of electromagnetic energy that can travel through the medium: a changing **voltage** on copper wire, **pulses of light** in a fibre-optic cable, or **radio waves** through the air. The left picture is an electrical signal on copper; the right one is light pulses in fibre. Turning data into a suitable signal, and back again at the receiver, is the physical layer’s core job.'],
    5: ['**Transmission** is communicating data by sending signals through a channel. A **signal** is **electromagnetic energy** that moves through the medium and carries information. The basic system has three parts: a **transmitter**, a **communication channel** and a **receiver**.',
        'The **transmitter** turns information into a signal suited to the medium and **injects energy** into the channel: a telephone turns your voice into electric current, a **NIC** turns bits into electric current or light pulses. The **receiver** takes the energy from the medium and turns it back into a usable form: the phone turns current back into sound, the NIC turns current or light back into bits. A NIC is both a transmitter and a receiver.'],
    6: ['**Analog data** is **continuous**: it can take any value in a range, like the exact loudness of a sound or the temperature. **Digital data** is **discrete**: it takes separate values, like text characters or whole numbers. The same split applies to signals. An **analog signal** is a smoothly varying wave that can have an **infinite number of values** in a range over time. A **digital signal** is a series of voltage pulses (a square wave) with only a **limited number of values**: it stays at one constant level, then jumps to another.',
        'Separately, a signal can be **periodic**, meaning it repeats the **same pattern** over identical time periods called **cycles**, or **aperiodic**, meaning it changes **without a repeating pattern**. These are two different classifications: analog/digital is about which **values** the signal can take, periodic/aperiodic is about whether its **pattern repeats** over time. So a signal can be periodic analog, aperiodic digital, and so on.'],
    7: ['Picture (a) is analog: a smooth wave that passes through every value between its highs and lows. Picture (b) is digital: flat steps that jump between a few levels. The rule to remember from the next slide: in data communication we commonly use **periodic analog** signals (a repeating sine wave, used as a carrier, for example in radio) and **aperiodic digital** signals (the bit stream of real data doesn’t repeat, because the data itself doesn’t).'],
    8: ['The slide repeats the rule on purpose because it is a classic MCQ: **periodic analog** and **aperiodic digital**. Why aperiodic digital? Because a digital signal carries real data such as 10110010…, which doesn’t repeat in a regular pattern. Why periodic analog? Because a pure repeating sine wave is easy to generate and can be changed (modulated) to carry information.'],
    9: ['**Frequency** is how fast a signal changes over time: the number of complete cycles per second, measured in **hertz (Hz)**. If the signal changes a lot in a short time, the frequency is **high**.',
        'If the signal changes slowly, over a long time, the frequency is **low**. Two extremes: a signal that never changes at all has a frequency of **zero**, and a signal that jumps instantly from one level to another has (in theory) an infinite frequency.'],
    10: ['**Frequency and period are inverses**: **f = 1/T** and **T = 1/f**. The **period** T is the time one cycle takes, in seconds; the **frequency** f is the number of cycles per second, in hertz. If one cycle takes 0.5 s, then 2 cycles fit into a second, so f = 2 Hz. Tutorial 3, Question 5 asks exactly this: f = 5 kHz = 5,000 Hz, so T = 1/5,000 = 0.0002 s = 200 µs.'],
    11: ['Worked example: home power is **60 Hz**, so T = 1/60 = 0.0166 s. To convert seconds to milliseconds, multiply by 1000: 0.0166 × 1000 = **16.6 ms**. So one cycle of mains power lasts about 16.6 ms. (In Saudi Arabia the mains frequency is also 60 Hz.)'],
    12: ['Going the other way: the period is **100 ms**, and the question wants kHz. Step 1, convert to base units: 100 ms = 0.1 s. Step 2, use the formula: f = 1/0.1 = **10 Hz**. Step 3, convert to the unit asked: 10 Hz = 10/1000 = **0.01 kHz**. Always do these three steps in that order, because dividing 1 by 100 (forgetting to convert ms) gives an answer 1000 times too small.'],
    13: ['A **simple signal** is a single **sine wave**, so it has exactly **one frequency**: every cycle is identical and takes the same time. A **composite signal** is made of **several sine waves with different frequencies** (and possibly different amplitudes and phases) added together, so its cycles don’t all look the same. Real signals, such as voice or a digital bit stream, are composite. This is Tutorial 3, Question 8.'],
    14: ['A digital signal represents bits as voltage levels: here **1 = a positive voltage** and **0 = zero voltage**. Most digital signals are **aperiodic**, because the data they carry doesn’t repeat, so frequency and period don’t describe them well. Instead we use **bit rate** (bits per second) and **bit interval** (the time one bit takes), defined on slide 16.'],
    15: ['A digital signal can use more than two levels. With **2 levels**, each level carries **1 bit** (low = 0, high = 1), so 8 levels sent in one second carry 8 bits: 8 bps. With **4 levels**, each level carries **2 bits** (00, 01, 10, 11), so 8 levels in a second carry 16 bits: 16 bps. The rule: with L levels, each level carries **log₂L bits**. 8 levels carry 3 bits, 16 levels carry 4. More levels means more bits per second without changing how fast the signal changes level.'],
    16: ['Two measures for digital signals. The **bit interval** is the **time needed to send one bit**, the digital version of the period. The **bit rate** is the **number of bits sent per second** (bps), the digital version of frequency. It is also called digital bandwidth, transmission speed or link capacity. They are inverses: **bit rate = 1/bit interval**. Tutorial 3, Question 1: bit interval = 50 µs = 0.00005 s, so bit rate = 1/0.00005 = 20,000 bps = **20 Kbps**.'],
    17: ['Worked example: bit rate = 2000 bps, so bit interval = 1/2000 = 0.0005 s. To convert seconds to microseconds, multiply by 10⁶: 0.0005 × 10⁶ = **500 µs**. Sanity check: if each bit lasts half a millisecond, 2000 of them fill one second.'],
    18: ['Why digital transmission is preferred: it is **cheaper** (digital chips are inexpensive), it can travel **longer distances** because repeaters regenerate a clean copy of the bits instead of boosting noise, it supports **security and privacy** through **encryption**, **noise can be removed** because the receiver only has to decide between a few levels, many signals can be combined by **digital multiplexing**, and both analog and digital data can be **processed** the same way once they are digital.'],
    19: ['No medium is perfect. Each one passes some frequencies well, **blocks** others and **weakens** others. Because a real signal is composite (made of many frequencies), the medium treats its parts differently, so the signal that comes out is never exactly the signal that went in. The figure shows a sharp square wave coming out rounded. This is the reason impairments exist.'],
    20: ['**Impairments** are the factors that make the **received signal different from the transmitted one**. There are three: **attenuation** (the signal gets weaker), **distortion** (the signal changes shape) and **noise** (unwanted signals get added). Learning what each one changes (strength, shape, or extra energy) is enough to identify it in any scenario.'],
    21: ['**Attenuation** is a **loss of energy**. As the signal travels, the medium resists it, and some of the energy is turned into heat (the slide’s example: a wire carrying a signal gets warm). So the signal gets **weaker with distance**. It also **increases with frequency**: higher-frequency signals lose strength faster, which is one reason high-speed links need shorter cables or better media.',
         'The fix depends on the signal. **Amplifiers** are used for **analog** signals: they boost the signal, but they also boost any noise in it. **Repeaters** are used for **digital** signals: they read the weak signal, work out which bits it carried, and **regenerate** a clean new copy. Tutorial 3, Question 2 asks what a repeater does and why it is needed: it regenerates a weakened or distorted digital signal so the data can travel farther without losing its form.'],
    22: ['**Distortion** means the signal **changes shape**. It happens to composite signals because each frequency component travels through the medium at a slightly different speed, so the components arrive at slightly different times and no longer add up to the original shape. The slide’s example is a video call with distorted images or audio. The key word in a question is “shape”: weaker is attenuation, reshaped is distortion.'],
    23: ['**Noise** is unwanted energy that gets **added** to the signal. **Thermal noise** comes from the **random motion of electrons** in the wire, which creates an extra signal that was never sent; it is always present. **Induced noise** comes from outside sources such as **motors and electrical equipment** (like a microwave or a large machine), which act as antennas sending interference into the cable. Tutorial 3, Question 9 is this case: errors caused by nearby electrical equipment are induced noise.',
         '**Crosstalk** is the effect of one wire on another: two wires lying beside each other, and one picks up the signal of the other. It is like hearing another conversation in the background of your phone call (Tutorial 3, Question 6). Twisting the wire pairs, as in twisted-pair cable, reduces it.',
         '**Impulse noise** is irregular, short spikes with **high amplitude**, caused by **power lines or lightning**. It is **very critical for digital signals**, and it is the **primary source of errors** in digital data communication, because a single spike can flip a whole run of bits.'],
    24: ['**Propagation time** is the time one bit needs to travel from one node to the other: **propagation time = distance / propagation speed**. It depends only on **how far** the bit goes and how fast signals move in the medium. It does not depend on the size of the message or the bandwidth.',
         'The **propagation speed** is the speed of light in the medium: **3 × 10⁸ m/s** in a vacuum, a little less in air, and much less in a cable, about 2/3 of that, so roughly **2 × 10⁸ m/s**. Tutorial 3, Question 4: 2,000 km = 2,000,000 m, so 2,000,000 / (2 × 10⁸) = 0.01 s = **10 ms**. Always convert km to m before dividing by m/s.'],
    25: ['The figure defines propagation time visually: the bit leaves the sender at time t1, travels distance d, and arrives at time t2. Propagation time = t2 - t1 = d / propagation speed.'],
    26: ['**Transmission time** is the time the sender needs to **push all the bits of a frame onto the link**, from the NIC into the medium: **transmission time = message size in bits / bandwidth (bps)**. It depends on **how big** the message is and how fast the link is, not on distance. Tutorial 3, Question 7: 1 MB = 8 Mb (multiply bytes by 8), and 8 Mb / 1 Mbps = **8 s**.',
         '**Queuing time** is the time each **intermediate device** (router, switch) holds the message in a queue before it can process and forward it. It depends on how busy the device is, so it changes all the time.',
         '**Latency** (delay) is the **total time to deliver a message** = **transmission time + propagation time + processing time + queuing time**. A simple way to remember what each term depends on: propagation = how **far**, transmission = how **big**, queuing = how **busy**, processing = how much work each device does.'],
    27: ['Before calculating, guess which delay will dominate: the message is small (2.5 KB) and the link is very fast (1 Gbps), but the distance is huge (12,000 km). Small message on a fast link means a tiny transmission time; long distance means a noticeable propagation time. The next slide confirms it.'],
    28: ['**Propagation time** = 12,000 km = 12,000 × 1000 m = 1.2 × 10⁷ m, divided by 2.4 × 10⁸ m/s = 0.05 s = **50 ms**. **Transmission time** = 2.5 KB = 2500 × 8 = 20,000 bits, divided by 10⁹ bps = 0.00002 s = **0.020 ms**. Propagation is 2,500 times bigger, so for a **short message on a high-bandwidth link, propagation dominates** and transmission can be ignored.'],
    29: ['Same distance and speed as before, but now a large message (5 MB) on a slow link (1 Mbps). The propagation time will be the same 50 ms, because distance hasn’t changed. Guess before you look: a big message on a slow link should take a long time to push onto the wire.'],
    30: ['**Propagation** is unchanged: **50 ms**. **Transmission** = 5 MB = 5,000,000 × 8 = 40,000,000 bits, divided by 10⁶ bps = **40 s**. Now transmission is 800 times bigger, so for a **long message on a low-bandwidth link, transmission dominates** and propagation can be ignored. Comparing slides 28 and 30 is the point: which delay matters depends on message size and bandwidth, not only on distance.'],
    31: ['A satellite link: A sends up to a satellite 36,000 km above, and the satellite sends down to B, another 36,000 km. So the total distance from A to B is **72,000 km** = 7.2 × 10⁷ m. Propagation time = 7.2 × 10⁷ / (3 × 10⁸) = **0.24 s** (each leg alone is 0.12 s). Signals in space travel at the vacuum speed of light, 3 × 10⁸ m/s.',
         'Transmission time = 2000 bytes = 16,000 bits, divided by 10⁹ bps = 0.000016 s = **16 µs**. Propagation (240,000 µs) is 15,000 times larger. This is why satellite links feel slow for interactive use, like calls and games, even when their bandwidth is high: the delay comes from distance, and more bandwidth can’t reduce it.'],
    32: ['Think of the link as a **pipe**. Its **cross-section** (how wide it is) is the **bandwidth**: how many bits can enter per second. Its **length** is the **delay**: how long a bit takes to get from one end to the other. Its **volume**, bandwidth × delay, is the **bandwidth-delay product**: the **number of bits that fit inside the link** at once, before the first one arrives. Tutorial 3, Question 10: 5 bps × 5 s = **25 bits**. A 10 Mbps link with a 2 ms delay holds 10⁷ × 0.002 = 20,000 bits. This matters later because a sender should keep that many bits in flight to use the link fully.'],
    33: ['Case 1: bandwidth 1 bps, delay 5 s. The sender puts one bit on the link each second, and each bit takes 5 s to arrive. When the first bit reaches the receiver, the sender has sent 5 bits, so **5 bits** are in the link: 1 × 5 = 5.'],
    34: ['Case 2: bandwidth 5 bps, same 5 s delay. Now 5 bits enter every second, so after 5 s the link holds **25 bits**: 5 × 5 = 25. These are the Tutorial 3 numbers. Comparing the two cases: the same pipe length, but five times wider, holds five times more bits.'],
    35: ['**Wavelength** is the **distance** a simple signal travels in **one period**, or the distance one cycle occupies in the medium. It depends on the **frequency** and on the **medium** (through its propagation speed): **wavelength = propagation speed × period = propagation speed / frequency**. Example: a 1 MHz signal at 2 × 10⁸ m/s has a wavelength of 2 × 10⁸ / 10⁶ = **200 m**. Higher frequency means shorter wavelength.'],
}

